SOC168 - Whoami Command Detected in Request Body

Note: These steps serve as a general guideline and may vary depending on the scenario.

1. Initial Triage
	• Alert Name: SOC168 - Whoami Command Detected in Request Body 
	• Event ID: 118 
	• Timestamp: Feb 28, 2022, 04:12 AM 
	• Affected Host: WebServer1004 
	• Severity: Medium (Security Analyst Level) 
Quick Summary:
An alert was triggered due to the presence of the whoami command in an HTTP POST request body, indicating a potential command injection attack targeting an internal web server.

2. Traffic & Payload Analysis
	• Source IP: 61.177.172.87 (External) 
	• Destination IP: 172.16.17.16 (Internal) 
	• Direction: Inbound 
	• Protocol: HTTPS (443) 
	• HTTP Method: POST 
	• URL: /video/ 
	• User-Agent: Legacy browser (MSIE 6.0) 
	• Device Action: Allowed 
Payload Insight:
	• The request body contains: whoami 
	• This suggests an attempt to execute system-level commands via input injection 
Key Observation:
	• The request was not blocked, increasing the likelihood of successful exploitation 

3. Threat Analysis
	• Attack Type: Command Injection 
	• Technique: Injecting OS commands through web input fields 
	• Goal of Attacker: 
		○ Identify current user (whoami) 
		○ Enumerate system (ls, uname) 
		○ Access files (cat) 
Threat Intelligence:
	• IP Owner: CHINANET Jiangsu Province Network 
	• ISP: chinatelecom.cn 
	• IP Type: Dynamic pool range 
	• Reputation: Malicious (VirusTotal, AbuseIPDB, Cisco Talos) 
Assessment:
This activity is consistent with real-world exploitation attempts, not benign scanning.

4. Investigation Actions
Steps Performed:
	• Reviewed alert details and request payload 
	• Verified traffic origin (external → internal) 
	• Checked threat intelligence sources for IP reputation 
	• Confirmed no internal planned test (email/simulation tools) 
	• Investigated endpoint logs via process/command history 
Findings:
The following commands were executed on the server:
	• whoami 
	• ls 
	• uname 
	• cat 
Key Insight:
	• These commands confirm successful command execution 
	• Indicates post-exploitation activity 

5. Escalation Decision
	• Escalation Required: Yes (Tier 2) 
Reason:
	• Confirmed command execution on endpoint 
	• Evidence of attacker interaction beyond initial access 
	• Potential for further compromise or lateral movement 

6. Conclusion
The alert represents a successful command injection attack against the internal web server.
Evidence shows that:
	• The attacker successfully executed the whoami command 
	• Additional commands (ls, uname, cat) indicate further system interaction 
This confirms that the system was compromised, not just targeted.

7. Mitigation & Response
Immediate Actions:
	• Isolated the affected host (WebServer1004) via Endpoint Security 
	• Prevented further attacker access 
Recommended Next Steps:
	• Perform full forensic analysis on the host 
	• Identify and patch the vulnerable web application 
	• Implement input validation and sanitization 
	• Deploy/strengthen Web Application Firewall (WAF) rules 
	• Monitor for lateral movement or persistence mechanisms 
	• Block or monitor malicious IP (61.177.172.87) 
